<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Bar Kokhba state</h1><p class="st-meta">132 CE – 136 CE · capital Betar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived Jewish state of Simon bar Kosiba, whose revolt against Hadrian was the last great Jewish rising against Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The revolt broke out in 132, probably provoked by Hadrian's plan to refound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> as the Roman colony <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>, and possibly by a ban on circumcision. Its leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kosiba</a>, was hailed as Bar Kokhba, 'son of the star', a messianic title reportedly endorsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabbi-akiva">Rabbi Akiva</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon</a> styled himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasi-title">Nasi</a> (prince) of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Israel</a> and dated documents by the 'redemption of Israel'. Coins overstruck on Roman issues showed the Temple facade and the slogan 'for the freedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'. Letters and contracts found in caves of the Judaean Desert, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahal-hever">Nahal Hever</a>, show a working administration that leased state land, collected produce and gave terse orders to commanders, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and Greek.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hadrian brought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-julius-severus">Julius Severus</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, who wore the rebels down with a war of sieges. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betar">Betar</a> fell in 135 or 136. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a> was devastated, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria-palaestina">Syria Palaestina</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 132 CE – 136 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:62.33%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>132 CE – 136 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Betar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Simon bar Kokhba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bar Kokhba revolt state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=134&amp;focus=bar-kokhba-state" aria-label="Bar Kokhba state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5586 1023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bar Kokhba state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5906 1279L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5906 1279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5906 1279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Betar. <a href="/atlas?year=134&amp;focus=bar-kokhba-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
